A||||6||wahoo4u||3800 swap||||3||
Z||000000||wahoo4u||01-19-2001||09:30 PM||||I have axcess to a 3800 from a 86 lesabre.  What i need from you guys and gals is what needs to be done in order to get it into my car.  Any certain modifications needed.  Motor mounts used.  Starter location.  Fuel system.  Anything and everything before i get started.  I have the option of using either my stock tranmission auto by the way or the tranny from the lesabre.  The engine tranny combo is only $150 and i need an engine.  To me it is too good of a deal to pass up.  Any info I can get would be very greatful.  Thanks for all your help in advance.  You guys and gals are great.||||reg||3
Z||000001||Black88GT||01-19-2001||09:37 PM||||I am by far an expert on 3.8 swaps, but from what I know, that engine is not a 3800, its a 3.8.  It would take just as much work to install it as a 3800, and your getting a much older engine.  So I would pass, it might look good for 150 but you will spend MUCH more then that to install it and your not getting a newer engine.<P>I'm sure someone can help you out more though.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||01-20-2001||09:12 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/38engine.jpg"> <BR>I installed this '87 3.8 in my Yellow '86 GT. Compared to a 2.8, the engine has great throttle response and tons of torque. Some people get excited if you call it a 3800 because GM didn't come out with the 3800 until '88. Most people will tell you to keep looking. If the '86 has a distributor instead of crank trigger & coil packs I would pass on it for clearance reasons. Otherwise I say go for it unless you can find a better deal on a newer engine. The older 3.8s are durable engines, and the power increase is enough to make it a worthwhile swap. If you keep the Fiero auto trans it will be an easy swap. If you want to use the 4t60 from the LeSabre, do a search on 4t60. I welded a new mounting plate on the cradle to accept the stock 3.8 front mount. The dogbone mounts to the bracket where the p/s pump was removed from. slightly modified 4cyl fuel lines work best, the rubber should be replaced with high pressure hose. stock 3.8 starter is used. Wiring is not too bad. Coil packs need to be relocated. Valve covers need to be switched F&lt;-&gt;R. Exhaust needs to be custom made. Stock Fiero exhaust can be used by either modifying the 3.8 crossover pipe or by biulding a new Y-pipe. You'll need a coolant fill adapter from Jeg's or Summit, or make your own like I did. Lower radiator hose from '87 Olds. See my car at <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/3800fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/3800fiero.html</A>   I made that site when I was trying to sell the car(It worked!). I got the engine for free and it actually was owned by a little old lady! and had 80,000 miles on it. I spent maybe another $200-300 for miscelaneous hoses, clamps, paint, and tuneup items. If you can do the work yourself it is not an expensive swap.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||elfiero||01-20-2001||09:23 AM||||Pass on this eng/trans combo as it is too<BR>old and the availiblity of newer and better<BR>3800s.the 3.8 you are looking at will have<BR>less HP,torque,poorer throttle response,and<BR>is more prone to oil leaks due to gasket<BR>design.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Tryxalon||01-20-2001||11:35 PM||||I don't know about the specific engine, but 3 years ago, I helped my my cousin find and buy a  87 Lesabre with the 3.8 engine. He paid $100 for it.  <P>Car has 308,000 miles on it, he swears it doesn't leak or use oil, says he gets 22 in town and about 29 on the highway at 70 mph.<P> That 185 hp and 210ft/lb  engine can propel the 3863lb car (door panel) 0-60 in 9.7 seconds (my stopwatch). Perhaps not that impressive, but a thousand pounds less of car should be a that much more impressive.<P>Now that he has graduated and has a good job, he can afford a newer car he says he wants to hold on to his Lesabre and see if he can hit 500,000 miles. That seems to be a testament to an "old engine". <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||01-21-2001||01:10 AM||||The 1986 3.8-liter engine only makes 150 horsepower and somewhere around 205-ft. lbs. of torque. The only thing it has going for it is torque. The 3800 engine is a much better engine and more reliable too. The first generation 3800 engines made 165 horsepower.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tryxalon||01-21-2001||01:11 PM||||In that case the 9.7 (say 10 second) 0-60 time in a 3800+ lb car is even more impressive with 20 less horsepower.<P>(took the hp/torque numbers from a Consumer's guide report on the 87 Lesabre) ||||reg||1
